 familiarize themselves with the Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement and the Employee Handbook (Section 12.1 Acceptable Use of Technology).\n\nRegarding reimbursement for the stolen laptop, it is unclear if the company will provide reimbursement under the standard incident response policy or if there are any additional requirements or restrictions that apply to remote workers using public Wi-Fi.\n\nTo resolve this situation, the user should report the incident and cooperate fully with the investigation. They may also want to review their internet reimbursement eligibility (or lack thereof) as stated in the Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1) \u2014 12.1 Acceptable Use of Technology.\n\nNote: Without more specific information about the company's policy language, it is difficult to provide exact financial responsibility for the stolen laptop. However, the user should expect reimbursement under the company's incident response policy, subject to any applicable penalties or fines.\n\n---\n**Sources:**\n1. Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement \u2014 4.2 Time Tracking for Non-Exempt Remote Employees\n2. Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement \u2014 3.2 Security Incident Response Procedure\n3. Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement \u2014 4.3 Remote Work Revocation Process\n4. Nexarion Remote Work Technology Supplement \u2014 Page 4\n5. Nexarion Solutions Employee Handbook (1) \u2014 12.1 Acceptable Use of Technology", "grounding_score": 0.7, "compliance_passed": true}
